Mike Piff made a suggestion regarding whether \section* etc.
should have an argument that forces a table-of-contents entry.

I'd suggest that such things be considered in the context of
     whether we have environments for front-matter, back-matter, etc., and
     what the "sectioning" commands are within such material

E.g.,, if one went
\begin{frontmatter}


\end{frontmatter}
it should be possible to arrange that "all top-level units in the
frontmatter environment (with the possible exceoption of list-of-tables and
list-of-figures) are to be listed in the table-of-contents", which would:
*  enforce house-style
*  save users from having to mess around specifying whether each
   individual unit (Preface, Acknowledgement, etc.) appears in the
   table-of-contents.

                                                                David Rhead

